    "html": "<p>Worth 10% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 10 points.</p><p>Purpose</p><p>Complete three dated field notes from Weeks 2 to 6. Start each note with a specific example from class, public example, or low-risk digital-system encounter. Use a course concept to explain the system rather than only describe it.</p><p>Each note must include</p><p>• The date and specific system or example.</p><p>• What the system did: sorted, ranked, watched, recommended, denied, or enabled.</p><p>• One course concept, explained in your own words.</p><p>• One piece of course or public evidence.</p><p>• Who benefits, who carries the cost, and what remains uncertain.</p><p>Format and boundary</p><p>Aim for 250 to 350 words or a 2 to 3 minute audio note per entry. Do not identify classmates or disclose private information. The dated example and your changing explanation are required process evidence. Generative AI may not create or rewrite entries.</p><p>How Set 1 is graded</p><ul><li>Specific dated evidence: 2.5 points.</li><li>course concept use: 3 points.</li><li>analysis of power and effect: 3 points.</li><li>reflection and communication: 1.5 points.</li></ul><p>Due</p><p>October 18, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p><h3>How to post your journal entries</h3><ul><li>Open this journal in Blackboard and find <strong>Entries</strong>. Start a separate entry for each required date or example.</li><li>Write directly in the journal editor. Put the entry number and date at the top. You do not need to upload a Word document.</li><li>If you choose an audio entry, attach the recording and a transcript, or add a Seneca-accessible recording link.</li><li>Select <strong>Post</strong>, then reopen the entry to confirm it is there. You may edit your entries before the due date. Only you and the teaching team can read this journal.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p>Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p><strong>Released:</strong> September 15, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p><strong>Due:</strong> October 18, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
