    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 20% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 20 points.</p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Read the scenarios and notice how you approach decisions about digital systems and unequal outcomes. Then use your choices and course learning to explain your thinking.</p><h3>Part A: 25 scenarios, worth 0 points</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">For each scenario, select one option using the numbered selection control. Choose the response that best matches where you would begin. There is no right or wrong selection. The options record your choices; they do not earn or lose points.</p><h3>Part B: four written responses, worth 5 points each</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Write 3 to 5 focused sentences for each response. Name the requested scenarios and option numbers. Explain your reasoning and apply the course idea or comparison requested. Your written responses carry all 20 points.</p><h3>Before you begin</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">You have one attempt and a 90-minute time limit. Start when you can work through the assessment in one sitting.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">No outside research is needed. Work independently. Do not use generative AI to choose or explain your responses.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">You may return to earlier questions while the attempt is open. Check every selection and written response before submitting.</li></ul><h3>How each written response is graded: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Specific scenarios and accurate references to your choices: 1 point.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear reasoning that answers every part of the prompt: 3 points. For Written response 3, this includes accurate use of the course idea.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Focused and understandable communication: 1 point.</li></ul><h3>How to complete and submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open the assessment and select <strong>Start attempt</strong>. For each scenario, use the numbered selection control to choose one option. You can change your selection while the attempt is open.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Write your answers in the response editors in this assessment. You do not need to upload a document.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Check each selection and written response before selecting <strong>Submit</strong>. Wait for the confirmation and keep your submission receipt.</li></ul><h3>Privacy and accessibility</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">You control what personal information you share. Remove identifying details about other people. Use readable headings and descriptive links. Add a text description for meaningful visuals, and captions or a transcript for audio or video. Your grade reflects your learning and reasoning; private disclosure and production effects do not earn extra marks.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Released:</strong> October 19, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due:</strong> October 23, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
