    "id": "case-file",
    "title": "3. Canadian Technology Hearing (20%)",
    "name": "Canadian Technology Hearing",
    "summary": "Investigate one documented Canadian technology case and explain it as if you were speaking at a public accountability hearing. Your job is to follow how a real decision was made, separate evidence from conclusions, and recommend a realistic response.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 20% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 20 points.</p><h3>Why this matters</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Investigate one documented Canadian technology case and explain it as if you were speaking at a public accountability hearing. Your job is to follow how a real decision was made, separate evidence from conclusions, and recommend a realistic response.</p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Choose one case provided in Blackboard, such as facial recognition in Canadian policing, correctional risk assessment after <em>Ewert v. Canada</em>, or a documented border technology.</p><h3>What to submit</h3><ol style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Evidence board:</strong> One readable page showing the system, institution, decision, affected group, unequal effect, accountable actor, and list of sources.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Hearing statement:</strong> A four-minute statement delivered live or submitted as a recording.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Follow-up response:</strong> Write approximately 200 words answering this question: Which piece of evidence most changed or complicated your understanding, and what would you need to know before acting on your recommendation? Refer to an exact page, section, or time in your source.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Sources:</strong> At least two verifiable sources, including one official, legal, court, oversight, or other primary source.</li></ol><h3>Evidence standard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Name the specific claim supported by each source.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Distinguish documented facts from your interpretation.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Name one important limit, uncertainty, or missing piece.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Do not impersonate or speak for affected communities.</li></ul><h3>How it will be graded: 20 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Source evidence: 6 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">System-chain analysis: 6 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Hearing response and remedy: 5 points</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Communication and list of sources: 3 points</li></ul><h3>Privacy, accessibility, and authorship</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use only the personal detail needed for the task. Equivalent written, audio, recorded, and accessible formats are available. Generative AI may be used only as a study aid. It may not write, rewrite, create, or narrate submitted work. If you use an AI tool while preparing, disclose the tool and the exact step. No AI detector will determine your grade.</p><h3>Use your own evidence trail</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Keep a dated note of your first question and one change you made after checking a source or receiving feedback. Include these notes in your evidence board or submission text. Use your course notes and original sources to explain your case. Do not invent a hearing, a source, a quotation, or a community response.</p><h3>Submitting your hearing statement</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">You may give your four-minute statement during the scheduled synchronous opportunity or attach a recording here. If you present live, identify the class date in your submission. Submit your evidence board, source list, and written follow-up in this same assignment by its due date.</p><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select <strong>Start attempt</strong>. Write or paste your written work into the <strong>Submission</strong> editor using the headings in these instructions. A Word upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Insert your required visual into the editor with a text description, or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select <strong>Submit</strong>, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Released:</strong> October 13, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due:</strong> November 6, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
