    "name": "Design the Repair Studio",
    "summary": "Choose one documented problem and design a practical response. Use the live studio for feedback, but submit an individual final design and rationale.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 20% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 20 points.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>BFS218, Fall 2026. Weight: 20 percent.</strong></p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Choose one documented problem and design a practical response. Use the live studio for feedback, but submit an individual final design and rationale.</p><h3>What to submit</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A short statement of the problem and a simple map showing who has power.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">State whether you will repair, reject, or replace the current approach.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A usable prototype such as a process map, wireframe, policy flow, or service design.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A rationale grounded in course concepts and evidence.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One visible change made after feedback.</li></ul><h3>What an effective response must do</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">A repair must change power, participation, accountability, or remedy. Improving technical accuracy alone is not enough when the institution or purpose remains harmful.</p><h3>How your work will be graded</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Strong means 85 to 100 percent of a criterion, Clear means 70 to 84 percent, Developing means 50 to 69 percent, and Limited means 0 to 49 percent.</p><h3>Diagnosis: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> The harm and power structure are precise and grounded in evidence.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The harm and main power relationship are clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> Diagnosis is broad or partly unsupported.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The design responds to an unclear or unsupported problem.</li></ul><h3>Community and power: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> Affected people have meaningful authority and missing voices are addressed.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> Participation and power are considered clearly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> Participation is mentioned but not built into the design.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The design reproduces the same decision structure.</li></ul><h3>Repair quality: 6 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> The prototype changes power, process, accountability, and remedy in a usable way.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The repair is practical and responds to the main harm.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> The repair is promising but incomplete or mostly technical.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The proposal does not address the diagnosed harm.</li></ul><h3>Iteration and rationale: 4 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> Feedback produces a visible, well-explained improvement grounded in course evidence.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> A clear change and rationale are shown.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> The change or rationale is thin.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> No meaningful iteration is shown.</li></ul><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Your grade is based on the evidence in your work. Personal disclosure, polished production, and technical effects do not replace course learning.</p><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select <strong>Start attempt</strong>. Write or paste your written work into the <strong>Submission</strong> editor using the headings in these instructions. A Word upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Insert your required visual into the editor with a text description, or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select <strong>Submit</strong>, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Released:</strong> November 23, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due:</strong> December 4, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
